      "headline": "The Three Bad Roommates in Your Mind",
      "keyInsight": "Imagine living with three sloppy roommates: one is blindfolded, one grabs everything in sight, and one falls asleep on the couch with the stove on. That is your mind under ignorance, avarice, and negligence. Ignorance obscures the plain truth right before your eyes. Avarice hoards every pleasant feeling like a miser collecting shiny pebbles. Negligence forgets to lock the front door, letting troubles walk right in. The joke is that we mistake these troublemakers for our trusted advisors. Spotting them in real time is the ultimate household cleanup.",
      "content": "Picture driving down a winding coastal highway on a clear afternoon. To stay safe, you need a clean windshield, both hands on the wheel, and steady attention on the road. If the glass is covered in thick mud, you are driving blind. That thick mud is ignorance. It hides the simple truth of what is happening right now.\n\nNext comes avarice, which behaves like a driver obsessed with collecting roadside billboards. Instead of watching the road ahead, you reach out the window to grab every pretty thing you pass. Greed wants pleasant sensations to freeze in place forever. But pleasant feelings are like warm bakery bread. They smell wonderful, but you cannot preserve the warmth by squeezing the loaf.\n\nFinally, negligence is taking a quick nap behind the wheel. You assume the car will steer itself through the cliffs. In daily life, mental laziness lets old habits run wild. U Pandita urges us to use sharp, continuous mindfulness as our alert navigator. Awareness wipes the windshield clean, loosens our frantic grip, and keeps both eyes on the road.",
