      "headline": "The Four Crooked Mirrors of the Mind",
      "keyInsight": "We walk around looking at life through four funhouse mirrors, convinced the distorted reflection is reality. We treat things that constantly change as if they are permanent statues. We chase sensations that bring stress, convinced they hold ultimate satisfaction. We polish an ego that does not exist like an antique trophy. And we mistake temporary shiny surfaces for deep purity. The hilarious twist of human existence is our stubborn insistence on buying round pegs for square holes. We know flowers wilt, yet we act heartbroken every single autumn. Real peace does not come from painting the wilting petals bright pink. It comes from finally putting down the cracked glasses and seeing things as they actually are.",
      "content": "Think about buying a brand-new sports car. For the first two weeks, you wash it with microfiber cloths. You park miles away from other cars so nobody scratches the paint. You genuinely feel that this shiny metal shell has brought lasting joy into your life. But three years down the road, there is a door ding, a coffee stain on the seat, and an expensive transmission repair bill. The machine never promised to stay brand new forever. Your mind simply glued an imaginary label of permanence onto a moving target.\n\nThis mental distortion is what the teacher calls 'vipallasa', or perversion of view. It is like walking through a desert, spotting a mirage of cold drinking water, and running toward it until you collapse from heat exhaustion. The mirage was never real water. Our mind regularly makes four classic blunders. It views the changing as permanent. It views the exhausting as satisfying. It views temporary processes as a solid 'me'. And it confuses surface flash with genuine purity.\n\nSeeing clearly through these illusions is not depressing at all. In fact, it is the biggest sigh of relief you will ever take. You stop demanding that ice cream never melt. You stop expecting weather to stay sunny on demand. When you stop asking temporary things to provide eternal happiness, life becomes remarkably easy to enjoy. You admire the sunset without begging the sun to freeze in the sky.",
